[SourceQuality "1"]

{At the end of a hard week the tension among the leaders was evident. Wood, with a difficult position against Brinck-Claussen, blundered a pawn away and resigned in 24 moves. Hempson, at move 13, left Stephenson a Knight fork to win the Queen. In my own game with Golombek, after winning two Rooks and Bishop for the Queen, I saw a “mate in 2” in the diagrammed position with} 64. Bg8+ $2 ({By playing} 64. R6g2 {, instead of the blunder 64 Bg8+, I could have won, for} Qxh3+ {loses against} 65. Rh2 {winning the Queen or mating, so White can rearrange his rooks onto the f-file thereafter securing safety for his king.}) 64... Kh8 {65 Rh6, but on looking again noticed with horror that his queen could take the rook. Realisation then dawned that a draw was inevitable for Black threatens 64...Qxh3 mate and there is no time to organize a defence against perpetual check. Source: BCM, September 1965, p263 (not sure if more moves were played after 64...Kh8)} 1/2-1/2

